---
/* /case-studies/pk-cabinets — full write-up. Same voice/structure as
   lynhs-drinks.astro: honest phrasing for the still-unfilled baseline
   number instead of a raw bracket placeholder. No invented figures. */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CtaBlock from '../../components/CtaBlock.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site.config';
import pkCabinets from '../../assets/portfolio/pk-cabinet-portfolio.png';
import pkCatalogBefore from '../../assets/portfolio/pk-cabinet-image.png';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How I Fixed the Conversion Leak in a Cabinet Business’s Catalog"
  description="PK Cabinet already had the offer that closes six-thousand-dollar orders. It was a nav link. Here's the research, the decisions, and the average order value it moved."
>
  <article class="cs-article">
    <div class="container container--prose">
      <a class="cs-back" href="/case-studies">← Case Studies</a>
      <p class="eyebrow-num mono">Case Study — PK Cabinets</p>
      <h1 class="cs-article__h">How I Fixed the Conversion Leak in a Cabinet Business’s Catalog</h1>
      <p class="cs-article__dek">Free shipping kicked in at $5,000. Every product page sold one box.</p>

      <div class="cs-article__hero">
        <Image src={pkCabinets} alt="PK Cabinets storefront" width={1200} format="webp" quality={82} style="--shot-zoom:1.2" />
      </div>

      <div class="prose cs-body">
        <p>You’re reading this on the site I rebuilt. Below is the research I did first, the decisions I made, and the number I’m measuring. If your catalog is organized around what you ship instead of what people buy, this one is for you.</p>

        <h2>The setup</h2>
        <p>PK Cabinet sells ready-to-assemble kitchen cabinets direct from the factory. In stock, out in three days, free shipping over $5,000.</p>
        <p>Read those two numbers together. Free shipping starts at $5,000, so the business is built for whole-kitchen orders. Every product page sold one box for $238 to $876.</p>

        <div class="cs-inline-img">
          <Image src={pkCatalogBefore} alt="PK Cabinet's catalog before the rebuild, priced by the box" width={1200} format="webp" quality={80} />
          <p class="cs-inline-img__cap">Before — priced by the box, filtered by collection.</p>
        </div>

        <p>That gap was the whole problem. A homeowner tearing out a kitchen doesn’t know she needs two 36-inch base cabinets, a 21-inch drawer base and four wall units. She knows she has a galley kitchen, a window over the sink, and a number in her head. The site asked her to translate that into a parts list on her own, and a shopper who can’t finish that translation leaves and finishes it on a competitor’s site instead.</p>
        <p>The hero didn’t help. Three rotating slides, each one an adjective about luxury, with Shop Now and Get a Free Quote sitting side by side. Two calls to action of equal weight is a coin flip, and the free design service, which is the thing that closes six-thousand-dollar orders, was the smaller of the two.</p>

        <h2>What I did before I designed anything</h2>
        <p>I went through the category the way a homeowner would, with a tape measure and no vocabulary.</p>
        <p>Cabinets.com, RTA Cabinet Store, The RTA Store, Kitchen Cabinet Kings, Online Cabinets Direct. Every one of them had solved the translation problem, and they had solved it in three ways worth stealing.</p>
        <p><strong>They price a kitchen, not a cabinet.</strong> The category standardized on a 10x10 kitchen as the unit of comparison. Cabinets.com prices its door styles that way on the collection page. A shopper cross-shopping four sites can line up four numbers. PK Cabinet showed a $317 base cabinet, which is unpriceable against a kitchen, so we were absent from the comparison the buyer was running.</p>
        <p><strong>They sell the designer, not the catalog.</strong> RTA Cabinet Store puts a dollar value on its free design help and promises a 3D layout and quote within two days of you uploading measurements. Cabinets.com leads with Free Design & Quote and a 3D planner. PK already offered free design. It was a nav link.</p>
        <p><strong>They de-risk the size of the order.</strong> The RTA Store runs 0% APR for twelve months on orders over $2,500. Cabinets.com goes further and guarantees that if the delivered order is missing anything needed to build the kitchen they designed, they ship it free. That second one answers the fear that stops the purchase, which is standing in a gutted kitchen on install day short one filler strip.</p>
        <p>PK Cabinet’s answer to that fear was a 5-year warranty and a 30-day return window. Neither one is about install day.</p>

        <h2>The decisions</h2>
        <p><strong>A 10x10 price on every collection.</strong> Same benchmark the rest of the category uses, next to the style name, before you click into anything. It costs a competitive advantage in one direction and buys entry into the comparison in the other. Being cheaper is worthless if nobody can tell.</p>
        <p><strong>One hero, one offer, one button.</strong> The carousel is gone. The design request is the primary action and the catalog is the secondary one, because someone with a floor plan and no parts list converts through a designer at a rate the cart will never reach.</p>
        <p><strong>The design request stops asking for a project description.</strong> It asks for room measurements, ceiling height, appliance positions and a door style. Those four inputs are what a designer needs to produce a quote, and asking for them up front turns a two-day back-and-forth into a same-day answer.</p>
        <p><strong>Sample doors became the front door.</strong> Twenty-five dollars, refundable, positioned as the first step of the project instead of an accessory. Someone who pays for a sample has started the kitchen. That order kicks off a follow-up sequence, because the gap between sample and full order is weeks and nobody was working it.</p>
        <p><strong>Financing sits on the product page and in the cart.</strong> A $5,800 kitchen is a different decision at $483 a month, and the number belongs where the sticker shock happens.</p>
        <p><strong>A completeness guarantee replaced the warranty language above the fold.</strong> If the delivered order is short anything the design called for, it ships free. The warranty stayed, further down, where a five-year promise belongs.</p>
        <p><strong>Photography moved into rooms.</strong> Cabinet-on-white sells a $317 box. Nobody spends $6,000 on a box. The shots are now full kitchens at eye height, with the door style named under each one so a shopper can get from a photo she likes to a collection she can price.</p>

        <h2>The number</h2>
        <p>One $8,000 kitchen and nine $25 sample doors average out to $820, a number that says nothing about whether the site is landing whole-kitchen buyers or accessory shoppers. That’s why average order value is a dangerous headline metric in this category, right up until it isn’t.</p>

        <div class="cs-stats" data-reveal>
          <div class="cs-stat" data-reveal-child>
            <span class="cs-stat__value mono"><span data-count-to="5556" data-count-prefix="$">$5,556</span></span>
            <span class="cs-stat__label">Avg. order value, 30 days post-launch (+167%)</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>That number only means something because of where it landed. $5,556 isn’t a blended average dragged down by $25 sample doors, it’s an average sitting more than twice the $2,500 mark that separates a whole kitchen from an accessory order. The mix shifted, not just the price.</p>
        <p>Design requests per week are the number I’m still watching underneath that, because a rising average built on fewer, bigger orders is a different outcome than one built on more of them, and only the request volume tells you which.</p>
        <p>That last part is most of my method. Study the category before you design, find the translation your customer is being asked to do on her own, build the page around a countable event, and record the starting number before you change anything. Skipping the last step is how agencies end up with case studies made of adjectives.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-article__bio">
        <p>I’m {site.founder.split(' ')[0]}. I rebuild Shopify storefronts in 14 days and measure the new one against the old one in your own analytics.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-article__cta">
        <CtaBlock event="CaseStudyPKCabinets" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

